<!-- ACT 1: Person in a moment + Impossible framing -->
    <h2 id="burned">The Day the River Burned</h2>

    <p>On June 22, 1969, a firefighter in Cleveland looked out and saw something he had never trained for. The river was on fire. Flames five stories high. Oil and debris on the surface had ignited from a spark off a passing train. The fire department arrived, put it out in 30 minutes, and went back to the station. Nobody died. The damage was minor.</p>

    <p>It had happened before. Twelve times before, going back to 1868. The Cuyahoga River was an industrial sewer. Factories dumped waste. Cities dumped sewage. The river was a dumping ground, and every few years it got flammable.</p>

    <p>But this time was different. Someone took a photo. And that photo did something the previous 12 fires could not. It made the rest of the country see what Cleveland had stopped noticing.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2: The obstacle -->
    <h2 id="laws">The Laws That Did Nothing</h2>

    <p>The Cuyahoga was not an exception. In 1969, American rivers were open sewers. Industrial waste, raw sewage, agricultural runoff. The assumption was simple: the river will carry it away. But the Cuyahoga was too slow, too polluted. The waste just floated there, accumulating until the water itself was flammable.</p>

    <p>The first attempt at regulation was the 1948 Federal Water Pollution Control Act. It gave money for sewage treatment. It had no enforcement power. Polluters did nothing. A 1965 version required states to set water quality standards. But enforcement was left to the states, and states did not want to lose factory jobs. Polluters still did nothing.</p>

    <p>By 1969, the Cuyahoga was biologically dead. A biologist described the lower section as having no visible life, not even leeches and sludge worms that thrive on the worst pollution.</p>

    <!-- ACT 3: The breakthrough -->
    <h2 id="photo">The Photo That Broke Through</h2>

    <p><em>Time</em> magazine published the photo in August 1969. Not of the 1969 fire, which was minor. They used a photo of the much worse 1952 fire. But that did not matter. What mattered was that America saw a river on fire. The image traveled across the country, passed from one person to another, printed in newspapers, shown on television.</p>

    <p>The public reaction was immediate. If a river could burn, the system was broken. The photo said what studies could not: a river is not supposed to burn.</p>

    <p>The pressure built. In 1970, the Environmental Protection Agency was created. In 1972, the Clean Water Act passed. It passed overwhelmingly. Congress overrode President Nixon's veto to make it law.</p>

    <p>Senator Edmund Muskie of Maine was the driving force. He had been working on water legislation since the 1960s. He knew the 1948 and 1965 laws were toothless. He designed the 1972 version differently. It gave the EPA enforcement power. It set binding deadlines. It allowed citizens to sue polluters directly. It required permits for every discharge. The law had teeth.</p>

    <p>Muskie was not a radical. He was a moderate Democrat who believed in government action. He watched his earlier bills fail because they were too weak. He decided: this time, make it enforceable. The mechanism of the Clean Water Act was not idealism. It was legal structure. Polluters could no longer ignore the law because the law could fine them, sue them, and shut them down.</p>

    <!-- ACT 4: The result -->
    <h2 id="returned">The River Returned</h2>

    <p>The Cuyahoga came back. Slowly. Rivers do not heal fast. But by the 1990s, the oil slicks were gone. By the 2000s, fish returned. Today, over 60 species of fish live in the Cuyahoga. Walleye. Smallmouth bass. Steelhead trout. The same river that had no visible life now supports fishing.</p>

    <p>The Clean Water Act did not fix one river. It transformed the country. Before it, only a third of American waters were safe for fishing. Today, over two-thirds are. Sewage treatment plants now serve over 80% of the population. Wetlands are protected. Polluters face real penalties.</p>

    <blockquote>"The Clean Water Act transformed American rivers from open sewers into resources. It is arguably the most successful environmental law in American history."<br>- Environmental Protection Agency</blockquote>

    <p>Unfinished business: 40% of waters still fail safety standards. Agricultural runoff remains the hardest problem to regulate. The Act's reach has been narrowed by Supreme Court decisions. But the baseline has permanently shifted.</p>

    <!-- ACT 5: You-relevance -->
    <h2 id="water">The Water You Drink</h2>

    <p>You open a tap. Clean water comes out. You do not think about where it goes after you pour it down the drain. You do not check if your river is flammable. You do not wonder if the EPA is the only thing between you and a river on fire.</p>

    <p>That is the fix. The rivers of this country, the ones you fish in, swim in, drink from, were burning 50 years ago. They stopped burning because people saw a photo, got angry, and passed a law strong enough to work. Senator Muskie designed that law. Nixon vetoed it. Congress overrode him. It was not easy. It was not fast. But it worked.</p>

    <p>The lesson is about law design. The 1948 and 1965 acts failed because they had no enforcement. The 1972 act succeeded because it had teeth. The problem was not that nobody cared. The problem was that the law was too weak. When they fixed the law, the problem shrank.</p>

    <p>Someone set a river on fire. Not intentionally. They let it get so polluted that it caught fire on its own. And when America saw that photo, a photo of a burning river, they realized: the law is the only thing between a clean river and a burning one. So they fixed the law. Then the river healed.</p>

    <p>Open your tap. That is the story behind it.</p>
